CORE
JmlMapStringKey
--function "JmlMapStringKey.useIt:()Ljava/lang/Object;" --jml-source src/main/java/JmlMapStringKey.java --axiomatic-collections --unwind 5 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
Regression for JML library-call recognition of
Map.containsKey(k) and Map.get(k).

Pipeline
--------
The two-operand library-call branch in resolve_jml_expr
detects jml_method_call(containsKey, [m, k]) and
jml_method_call(get, [m, k]) on Map / HashMap / Set /
HashSet receivers, then emits the equivalent inline
expression that --axiomatic-collections produces for
normal-code calls:

  pack_index(m, k) =
    bitor(shl(receiver_int, 32), key_int)

  containsKey(k) → _kv[pack_index(m, k)] != null
  get(k)         → _kv[pack_index(m, k)]
  contains(o)    → _set[pack_index(s, o)]

The receiver bits are the pointer's bottom 32 bits cast to
uint32 then up-extended; the key bits use value-semantic
packing for primitive int keys and pointer-identity for
reference-typed keys.

This regression uses String keys (reference-typed). The
resolver builds the same pointer-identity packing the
normal-code lowering uses, so the JML's
  //@ requires m.containsKey(k);
clause assumes the same _kv slot the body's m.get(k) reads,
and the ensures \result != null follows.

Known limitation: int-key Maps need a different packing
strategy because JBMC's autobox pattern detection at the
PUT/GET site uses pointer-identity on the boxed Integer,
while a JML int key would naturally pack value-semantically.
The two don't match and the assertion fires. Workaround
is to use a Map keyed on a reference type. Closing the
int-key gap is a separate piece of work in
java_bytecode_axiomatic.cpp.
